  q(TRANS,'Which bacterium is commonly used to transfer new genes into plant cells?','Agrobacterium tumefaciens',['Escherichia coli','Bacillus subtilis','Streptococcus pneumoniae'],'A','Its Ti plasmid naturally transfers DNA into plant chromosomes, which scientists adapted for engineering crops.',ST),
  q(TRANS,'Which device shoots tiny metal particles coated with DNA into plant cells?','A gene gun (biolistics)',['A centrifuge','A thermal cycler','A spectrophotometer'],'B','The gene gun is used for cells that are hard to transform with Agrobacterium.',BR),
  q(TRANS,'Besides an origin and a selectable marker, what does an expression vector need to make a protein?','A promoter and a ribosome binding site near the gene',['A centromere','A telomere','An intron in every gene so bacterial cells can splice the mRNA'],'C','The promoter starts transcription and the ribosome binding site starts translation in bacteria.',BR),
  q(PCR,'In qPCR, how does SYBR Green dye produce a signal?','It fluoresces when bound to double-stranded DNA',['It cuts the DNA','It glows only with RNA','It binds only to single primers and glows when they are free in solution'],'D','SYBR Green binds any double-stranded DNA, so a melt curve is used to check product specificity.',BR),
  q(PCR,'How is the annealing temperature usually chosen for a pair of primers?','About 3 to 5 °C below the primers’ melting temperature',['Exactly at 95 °C','At room temperature','About 20 °C above the melting temperature'],'A','Too low an annealing temperature causes nonspecific binding, and too high prevents binding.',BR),
  q(PCR,'Researchers know a protein’s amino acid sequence but not the gene’s exact DNA code. Which primers may be used?','Degenerate primers',['Random hexamers only','Fluorescent probes','Primer dimers'],'B','Degenerate primers are mixtures that cover the different codons that could code each amino acid.',BR),
  q(PCR,'Which method splits a PCR reaction into thousands of tiny partitions to count DNA molecules directly?','Digital PCR',['Gel electrophoresis','Nested PCR','Hot-start PCR'],'C','Counting positive and negative partitions gives an absolute count without a standard curve.',BR),
  q(PCR,'A qPCR standard curve gives an efficiency of 98%. What does this mean?','The product nearly doubles each cycle, which is acceptable',['The reaction failed','There was no product','The primers bound to many wrong sites and made extra products'],'D','Efficiencies between about 90% and 110% are generally considered acceptable.',BR),
  q(PCR,'Why is a housekeeping gene, such as GAPDH, measured in gene expression qPCR?','It serves as a steady reference to normalize results',['It is the gene of interest','It causes disease','It replaces the need for primers'],'A','Normalizing to a reference gene corrects for differences in the amount of starting RNA.',BR),
  q(PCR,'Why might DMSO be added to a PCR reaction for a GC-rich template?','It helps separate strands and reduces secondary structures',['It feeds the polymerase','It stains the DNA','It replaces the magnesium ions that the polymerase needs as a cofactor'],'B','GC-rich DNA has three hydrogen bonds per pair and forms stable hairpins that block copying.',BR),
  q(PROT,'Which type of inhibitor binds an enzyme away from the active site and lowers Vmax?','A noncompetitive inhibitor',['A competitive inhibitor','A substrate','A cofactor'],'C','Adding more substrate cannot overcome noncompetitive inhibition.',ST),
  q(PROT,'What does Vmax describe for an enzyme?','The maximum reaction rate when the enzyme is saturated with substrate',['The pH at which the enzyme is denatured','The size of the enzyme','The number of substrate molecules needed to fully denature the enzyme'],'D','At Vmax every active site is busy, so adding more substrate does not increase the rate.',ST),
  q(PROT,'Why are protease inhibitors added to cell lysis buffers?','To prevent enzymes released from cells from breaking down the target protein',['To make proteins fold','To stain proteins so their bands can be seen after the gel is run','To speed up digestion'],'A','Once cells are broken open, proteases can quickly destroy the protein being purified.',BR),
  q(PROT,'Which bacterial proteins are used to purify antibodies because they bind the Fc region?','Protein A and Protein G',['Albumin and casein','Actin and myosin','Insulin and glucagon'],'B','Affinity columns with Protein A or G capture antibodies from serum or cell culture media.',BR),
  q(PROT,'Misfolded proteins that cause other proteins to misfold, as in mad cow disease, are called:','Prions',['Enzymes','Antibodies','Chaperones'],'C','Prions cause fatal brain diseases and are very hard to destroy by normal sterilization.',ST),
  q(PROT,'Which method freeze-dries proteins so they can be stored as a stable powder?','Lyophilization',['Dialysis','Salting out','Electrophoresis'],'D','Removing water slows breakdown, and many vaccines and enzymes are stored this way.',BR),
  q(PROT,'Which chemical is used at high concentration to unfold proteins by disrupting hydrogen bonds?','Urea',['Glycerol','Sucrose','Sodium chloride at low strength'],'A','Urea and guanidine are denaturants often used to study folding or to solubilize protein clumps.',BR),
  q(IMM,'A small molecule, such as penicillin, triggers antibodies only after it attaches to a larger protein. What is it called?','A hapten',['An adjuvant','A toxoid','A cytokine released by T cells'],'B','Haptens are too small to start an immune response alone, which helps explain some drug allergies.',ST),
  q(IMM,'A snakebite victim receives antivenom made of antibodies. What type of immunity is this?','Passive immunity',['Active immunity','Innate immunity','Herd immunity'],'C','Passive immunity works right away but fades because the body did not make its own antibodies.',ST),
  q(IMM,'Which type of vaccine uses a live but weakened form of a virus?','Attenuated vaccine',['Inactivated vaccine','Toxoid vaccine','Subunit vaccine'],'D','Attenuated vaccines, such as MMR, give strong, long-lasting immunity but are not given to some immunocompromised people.',ST),
  q(IMM,'Which T cells directly kill virus-infected cells?','Cytotoxic (CD8) T cells',['Helper (CD4) T cells','B cells','Red blood cells'],'A','Cytotoxic T cells recognize infected cells and trigger them to die.',ST),
  q(IMM,'Which part of the immune system responds quickly and in the same way to many different germs?','Innate immunity',['Adaptive immunity','Memory B cells','Vaccine-induced immunity'],'B','Innate immunity includes skin, inflammation and phagocytes, while adaptive immunity is specific.',ST)
